[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Object Lifecycle, Immutable Objects, and Defensive Copying in Java"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Understanding how objects are created, managed, and eventually destroyed in Java is fundamental to writing robust, memory-efficient applications. This tutorial explores the object lifecycle, the power of immutability, and how defensive copying protects your internal state from unintended modifications."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. The Java Object Lifecycle"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Every object in Java goes through a well-defined lifecycle managed by the Java Virtual Machine (JVM). Understanding these phases helps prevent memory leaks and performance bottlenecks."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Creation: Memory is allocated on the heap using the 'new' keyword, and the constructor initializes the state."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use: The object is referenced and manipulated by application threads."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Unreachability: The object loses all active references, making it eligible for garbage collection."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Destruction: The Garbage Collector reclaims the memory occupied by the unreachable object."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Immutable Objects"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"An immutable object is an object whose internal state cannot be changed after it is constructed. Immutability simplifies concurrent programming because immutable objects are inherently thread-safe."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"public final class UserSession {
    private final String username;
    private final long loginTime;

    public UserSession(String username, long loginTime) {
        this.username = username;
        this.loginTime = loginTime;
    }

    public String getUsername() {
        return username;
    }

    public long getLoginTime() {
        return loginTime;
    }
}"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"To make a class truly immutable, ensure the class is declared 'final', all fields are 'private' and 'final', there are no setter methods, and any mutable objects referenced internally are handled securely."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Defensive Copying"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"If your class contains mutable fields (such as 'Date' or arrays) or accepts them in constructors and returns them in getters, callers can modify the internal state of your object. Defensive copying prevents this by creating new copies of objects during input and output."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"import java.util.Date;

public final class Booking {
    private final Date bookingDate;

    public Booking(Date bookingDate) {
        // Defensive copy on construction
        this.bookingDate = new Date(bookingDate.getTime());
    }

    public Date getBookingDate() {
        // Defensive copy on return
        return new Date(bookingDate.getTime());
    }
}"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"By applying defensive copying alongside immutable class designs, you ensure encapsulation is maintained, preventing external code from corrupting your internal object lifecycle state."}]
